camps.idea:
Every piece casts what it means. WORLD: the flat roofs of north India on 21 December, the winter solstice, when the sun stays lowest and the shadows are the longest of the year. The low sun rakes across lime-washed terrace parapets in palette colours: Jodhpur sky blue, pista, saffron, terracotta and coral-deep, with plum for the one dusk card. The real piece, built from its listing photo as reference 1 and exactly right in construction, does one of these:
- stands on a bone-white plaster ledge;
- hangs from one small brass nail;
- runs over a parapet edge or lies on the parapet top;
- is worn by one hand or forearm.

For its first few millimetres the piece keeps its own true contact shadow. After that, its long cast shadow stops being its shape and becomes its meaning:
- two stones throw two people holding hands;
- the butterfly ring's shadow flies off the finger;
- one two-turn coil throws a dozen bangles;
- the gold-tone snake chain throws a cobra carrying one jewel of light;
- the braided hoops throw a circle of sangeet dancers;
- the toggle throws a big old house key (the ring is its bow, the bar its shaft);
- the two-tone Duo throws the wedding knot, tassels and all;
- the Droplet cuff throws two hands that almost touch;
- the dome hoop, at dusk, throws the moon instead of a shadow;
- the Verdant band, on a grandmother's hand, throws a family tree whose leaves are its own green light.

The ten shadows fall into six families: two of people, two of bodies, two creatures, two objects, one sky and one tree. So no two cards read alike at 200 px. Each meaning is generated in the plate as a cast silhouette: sharp at the piece, softening with distance, plaster texture showing through it, deep cocoa, no faces. It never redraws or replaces the piece. The camp allows itself one impossibility: the meaning rises and grows. Its colour, softness and direction stay true to the sun.

LAW OF THE WORLD: the light counts, the shadow means. Every object line is an honest count: TWO STONES. ONE BUTTERFLY. TWO TURNS. ONE STONE. TWO HOOPS. ONE TURN. SIXTEEN KNOTS. TWO ENDINGS. ONE MOON. ONE RING. Every type-shadow under it says what that count means to one named person on her December list. Further rules:
- One jewel in the light. Walls and sun only, never water.
- The sun is real. Each card carries its true hour and the sun's true height over Delhi on 21 December, from 16 degrees at 3:58 PM down to 1 degree at 5:21 PM. So the shadows really do lengthen as she swipes.
- The size line jokes about what the shadow adds, and the joke grows: SHADOW / FLIGHT / THE OTHER ELEVEN / COBRA / DANCERS / SECRETS / BHAIYA / THE OTHER 1,740 KM / GARDEN NOT INCLUDED. Card 9 flips it: MOON INCLUDED · STARS NEXT DECEMBER.
- Where light passes through a shadow in the plate (the crystal's caustic, the cobra's jewel, the green leaves), the type carries the same light.
- Card 9 is the planned inversion. It is the only piece that throws light instead of shadow, and the only object line that counts the light (ONE MOON.).
- MOTION (cards 1, 2, 5 and 9): the sun drags lower and the shadow stretches from the piece's true outline into its meaning, while the piece stays pixel-locked. The type does the same: the object line's own true shadow (the same words, skewed) re-letters itself into the meaning.
  - On card 5 the dancers turn to a dhol-tasha beat while the hoops never move.
  - On card 9 the moon rises up the dusk wall.

camps.insight:
Indian families rarely say it out loud; they hand it over. December is the month she hands it over to everyone at once. The month with the longest shadows of the year is also the month with the longest gift list: weddings, sangeet, Christmas, the office Secret Santa, New Year, the cousins. PARCHHAI gives each slow piece a sentence she would never say aloud ('tum aur main', 'door hokar bhi paas', 'your secret's safe') and lets the shadow say it. Each piece then answers 'what do I give whom' before she has asked. Ten cards name nine different people, so the camp reads as one gift list, and the list is the BUY3 bundle.

THE COMMERCIAL INSIGHT, from our own account:
- The tested winners show one large piece whose own colour is the loudest thing in a calm frame (ruby anaar, F2).
- The losers had props that outweighed the piece (kandil lanterns) or pale-on-pale frames.
- A shadow is the only prop with no colour and no stuff. It can fill the whole wall and still never compete with the jewel. So the piece stays the brightest and most saturated thing in every frame, while the idea gets to be enormous.

Low hard winter sun is also the one light that makes these slow pieces look their price:
- every wire of the Woven braid carries its own highlight;
- a band of light moves across the tiger eye;
- green cubic zirconia throws green light;
- mirror-polished domes and teardrops blaze.

Two winners on the 200-ad board back the devices:
- Number 1 (a sun arc from 10 AM to 5 PM over the product) shows that a time-of-day arc stops the thumb. So the sundial hour is a proven stopper, not decoration.
- Number 82 (a product drawn as a padlock, 'locked in') shows that a clasp read as a lock is a pun people get instantly. The Toggle's key shadow uses it.

Shigeo Fukuda, and Tim Noble and Sue Webster, made shadow art. No Indian jewellery brand has used it, and no headline has ever cast its own shadow under the same sun as the photograph.

The pains are written in the buyer's own words, so the ad is something she recognises before it is something she admires:
- the ₹1,000 Secret Santa limit (the Toggle is ₹999);
- the new bhabhi whose taste you don't know yet;
- 'she's in Bengaluru, you're in Delhi';
- 'first December together, zero ideas'.

ads.risk:
Four risks:
- the coil read as 3 turns or as separate bangles (because of the 3-wire overlap between the caps);
- extra bangles leaking onto the real arm;
- the twelve shadow bangles merging into one tube;
- the copy breaking the rule: body copy never says triple, and the product name appears only on the F2 card.

Check the turn count at full resolution against photos 01 and 03.

Stock: gold tone is exactly at 20, so this card pauses if it drops below.

Hedge: generate the arm with its ordinary shadow, then stack eleven cocoa multiply ring shadows up the shadow forearm in the edit, 1 px apart.

Four risks:
- the stone growing past 10 mm;
- a jump ring or halo appearing;
- the snake chain drifting to herringbone;
- the cobra reading as a real snake or a snake-shaped chain.
Check the bail-to-basket join against reference 2.

Stock floor: the gold-tone Serpentine has 20 units. If it drops below 20, swap card 4 to Verdant Cushion Studs (30 units, pre-order, no claims, ₹1,099). Its object line becomes 'TWO GREEN STONES.' (the pair, one green cushion each; 'ONE STONE.' would be a false count on a cluster stud). Give it a sunburst shadow with the type-shadow 'the sun, for you;' so the thread still reads, with the lit point moving to the dot of the semicolon. Its callouts carry listing facts only: '13 MM ACROSS' / 'green cubic zirconia, 8 mm cushion' and 'HALO OF CLEAR STONES' / 'rhodium silver tone, pre-order'. No waterproof or tarnish word, and no water. Its link headline becomes 'Two green stones. ₹1,099'.

Four risks:
- the braid smoothing into a tube or twist;
- the hoops going oval or past 30 mm;
- the dancers reading as a flower or a cog at 200 px;
- Kling rotating the hoops along with their shadow.

Hedge: do the turn in the edit over a locked still. Keep the dancers' heads outward and skirts flaring so the circle reads as people.

Four risks:
- the knot connectors dropping out so the links hook straight into each other (the card's main morph);
- the silver-tone links warming to gold in the low sun;
- the alternation breaking into runs;
- the cloth shadow reading as a necktie (the founder twice rejected a silk knot that read as a tie).
Count 17 links and 16 knots, and check the tone order against photo 01.

Stock: 17 units, under the 20 floor. Run only under the owner's slow-list waiver, with capped spend, and pause at 10.

Hedge: if the knot reads as a tie, build it as an SVG silhouette with all four tasselled ends trailing outward and downward.

Four risks:
- the two ends turning symmetrical (two balls or two teardrops);
- the gap closing into a bangle;
- the cuff falling flat instead of standing on its curve;
- the two hands merging into one.

Stock: gold tone has 8 units and silver tone 6, both under the 20 floor. Run only under the slow-list waiver, with capped spend. When gold tone sells out, re-shoot in rhodium silver tone from its own photo 04 on the same pista wall.

Zero-credit variants on the same picture, as kicker-only headline variants: 'SHE'S IN PUNE. YOU'RE IN PATNA.' with 'THE OTHER 1,400 KM'; 'SHE'S IN MUMBAI. YOU'RE IN KOLKATA.' with 'THE OTHER 1,650 KM'. Re-check each straight-line distance before printing.

Hedge: composite the two hands as SVG silhouettes, one from each end's traced shadow, with the gap held at the cuff's width.

Four risks:
- the reflected crescent reading as a painted moon or a lamp;
- the dome swelling past 16 mm (the account's known scale drift for this piece);
- the dome evening out into a plain tube;
- the hinge wire getting lost, so the C closes.

Two hoops side by side must never read as a pair of eyes, so the second hoop lies on its side.

No claims (pre-order, no claim tags), no base metal named, no ship-now wording. The word 'huggie' appears only inside the product title on the card.

Hedge: generate the plate with the hoops in the slot of sun against a clean dusky wall. Then add the crescent in the edit as a soft screen-blend warm gradient, brightest at its lower curve, with the plaster texture lifted through it.

Five risks:
- ageing the hand without warping its anatomy;
- the S-wave straightening into a row of ovals, or closing into a halo around each oval;
- the open back closing into a full eternity band;
- the green light spots reading as painted leaves;
- the orange last sun warming the rhodium to gold (the blue-sky fill is named to hold it cool).

Copy never says emerald, diamond, 'one size fits all' or eternity outside the name.

Hedge:
1. Generate a worn plate with the hand's ordinary shadow.
2. Grow the canopy in the edit from the traced shadow fingers as a cocoa silhouette.
3. Place green radial light spots by hand (screen, 30-50%).

