camps.idea:
WORLD: Naira's own jewellery counter at night, opened the way every Indian shop opens: the rolling shutter rattles up, the tube light ticks on twice, and the rate board flickers to life. It is Naira's own counter, not someone else's shop and not an imitation counter. A glass-topped counter, one hard spot from above, one cool tube-light streak in the glass, deep cocoa darkness, and the honest instruments an Indian jeweller uses to prove a price: a velvet pad, the night safe, an antique hand balance, a pocket scale, a steel ring-sizing cone, the paper packet and the cotton wool a small piece is wrapped in, a carbon-copy estimate pad, and (card 8) the flower-seller's brass pan from the mandi next door. Above the counter hangs Naira's red dot-matrix rate board, the board every Indian jewellery shop has. Every other board climbs all wedding season; Naira's never moves: AAJ ₹699 / KAL ₹699. The pieces are priced by the piece, never by the gram, and THOK BHAAV (the wholesale rate every mother already uses at the mandi) for any three is BUY3, 20% off.

DEVICE: every card pairs the board with one honest instrument that grows out of its own picture:
- the scale's readout (listed grams only);
- the cone's size ticks (listed sizes only, each with its standard inside diameter);
- tag strings (construction facts);
- ghost slots in the empty pan (the two pieces that balance it);
- a torn estimate slip (the BUY3 sum);
- a jeweller's string tag (the relief line and the price);
- the real sailor ring standing in as the 0 of '20%';
- a board that cannot hold what a look is worth (its lit dots overflow the frame).
Each instrument carries at most three facts and is never a spec table; infographics earned clicks, not purchases, in our own account. No two cards share a stage or a camera: low hero on glass, top-down velvet, inside the safe looking out, worn from behind, three-quarter balance, the scale pan, an upright cone, the top-down mandi pan, a cotton nest, a bill pad. Maroon appears only twice.

PIECE FIRST: one product per picture, the loudest colour in the frame, 40-55% of the frame width on product-only cards. The board's saffron glow is the only orange light and never lands on the piece.

RULES:
- Never print a metal rate, write 'gold' alone, or show a struck or compare-at price. In motion, no number other than the listed price ever holds on screen.
- No shop names, hallmarks, tags or digits are generated in the picture. Every letter, digit, LED dot and the shutter are HTML.
- Grade neutral, so oxblood paper or velvet never tints rhodium or stones. No pearls in this camp.
- 'Kal bhi yahi' is a price promise: run it only if the owner holds these ten prices for the whole flight.

MOTION: card 1 is animated (Kling plate plus HTML): the shutter rattles up, the tube light ticks on, the board's price fields roll upward like a live rate update and clunk back onto exactly the same ₹699, and THOK BHAAV scrolls in. Cards 2, 5, 6, 7 and 10 have cheap HTML-only Reels cuts (a digit flip, ghost slots filling, an LCD count-up, ticks drawing on, a slip writing itself).

